. *********************************
. *** Apply sample restrictions ***
. *********************************
. 
. * We estimate the following regressions using a sample restricted to responde
> nts with exactly one child.
. * Note: There was a typo in the preliminary Stata code provided in the PAP. A
> lthough the verbal description 
. * of the sample restriction was correct, the code mistakenly included `parity
> 2==1`, which would select 
. * respondents with at least two children—contradicting the intended analytic 
> sample.
. 
. 
. keep if parity2!=1 // Hypotheses 1, 2, 3, 4
(200,410 observations deleted)

. 
. * Restrict the analyses to cohabiting couples 
. * keep if cohab==1 // Hypothesis 3
. 
. keep if !missing(left, daughters, syear, phrf) & phrf!=0
(449,938 observations deleted)

. 
. count
  12,775

. 
. *********************************
. *********************************
. 
. * HYPOTHESIS TEST FOR H2
. 
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         * We assume that the causal effect of having a daughter is identified
>  in a sample of parents of a single child without further adjustment (see PAP
> )
.         * Since information on variable "left" is available in multiple surve
> y years, we pool over and fully stratify by wave
.         * !! NOTE THIS IS THE AVERAGE CAUSAL EFFECT IN THE POPULATION OF PARE
> NTS OF A SINGLE CHILD !!
.         * Accordingly, we run a linear regression analysis with left [plh0004
> ] as dependent variables, assuming it can be treated as linear-continous,
.         * and the dummy variable daughter as independent variable in a sample
>  of respondents with a single child (parity2!=1)
.         * We apply weights cross-sectionally calibrated weights (phrf)
.         * To account for non-independence of observatons, we cluster standard
>  errors at the household level
.         * The null we test against is that the effect of having daughters is 
> <=0 
.         * I.e., we have to consider a one-sided test 
.         * For consistency with the PAP and between hypothesis tests, we emplo
> y the same method here, although not strictily necessary
.         * !! NOTE THAT AT AN A PRIORI CONFIDENCE LEVEL OF 0.05 WITHOUT PREDER
> MINED SIGN OF THE EFFECT, THE CONFIDENCE LEVEL BECOMES 0.025
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
.         * The below code follows the conception outlined in the PAP
.         * Note that the sample restriction is already implemented in the data
>  preparation and therefore omitted for redundancy
.         *********************************************************************
> *****************************************************************************
> ***************
. 
. 
.         *obtain point esttimates for daughter effects
.         regress left daughters##syear [pw=phrf], vce(cluster cid)
(sum of wgt is 35,214,566.55)

